## Approach

I computed live-in and live-out sets for every block by backward dataflow, iterating until the sets stabilized. Then, for each definition, I added an edge from the defined variable to every other variable live immediately after it.

**Stabilized live sets (key ones):**
- live_out(B0) = {a,b,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,l}
- live_out(B1) = {a,b,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,l}; live_in(B1) = {a,d,e,f,g,h,i,l}
- live_out(B2) = {a,d,f,g,h,l}; live_in(B2) = {a,b,d,e,f,g,h,j,l}
- live_out(B3) = {a,c,d,g,i,j,k,l}; live_in(B3) = {a,d,f,g,h,l}
- live_out(B4) = {a,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,l}; live_in(B4) = {a,c,d,g,i,j,k,l}
- live_out(B5) = {a,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l}
- live_out(B6) = {a,b,c,d,e,g,h,i,j,k,l}; live_in(B6) = {a,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l}
- live_out(B7) = {a,b,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l}
- live_out(B8) = {a,b,d,f,g,h,i,j,l}
- live_out(B9) = {a,b,d,f,g,h,i,j,l}
- live_out(B10) = {a,b,d,e,f,g,h,i,l}
- live_out(B11) = {}, live_in(B11) = {b,e,i}

**Interference from definitions:** Every defined variable's live-after set was checked. Walking through all 12 variables, each one picks up an edge to all of the other 11. For example:
- `c = 1` in B1 has `g` live after it, since `g = 1` comes later... more precisely, `c` is read by `if c` after `g = 1`, so `g` and `c` interfere through `g = 1`.
- `k = 1` in B8 has `a` live after it, because `a` is read in B3 (`b = a + 1`) and B3 is reachable from B8.
- `e = 1` in B5 has `a`, `d`, `f`, `g`, `h`, `i`, `j`, `k` live after it.

Spot-checks confirmed the pairs that looked least obvious (c–g, k–a, d–c, e–k, f–g). Every pair of the 12 variables has at least one defining point where the other is live, so the graph is complete.

**Result:** the interference graph is the complete graph K12 on {a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l}, which gives 66 edges.

